  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nLow pressure is now forecast to track a little further south\nacross southern PA today, so this will keep the bulk of the\nheavier rainfall outside our CWA. Still on track to see a half\ninch to an inch of rain across the Poconos and into the Southern\nCatskills, but much less than what previous forecasts were\nadvertising. With the lower rainfall totals, WPC has removed any\nmention of excessive rainfall. \n\nRainfall will come to an this evening behind the departing low. \nA very dry air mass will advect into the area from Canada late \ntonight into Monday. While there could be some patchy fog, it \nwill most likely dissipate as drier air mixes down toward the \nsurface. Overnight lows will be quite cool, dropping into the \n40s to low 50s areawide.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nDry high pressure is expected to remain overhead on Monday.\nThere is another weak shortwave that could bring a round of rain\nshowers on Tuesday, but with the stalled front likely ending up\nfurther south than previous forecast, the best chances for rain\nshowers will be in NEPA. The remains quite a bit of uncertainty\nin the model guidance, with the GFS lifting the stalled boundary\nfurther north with higher rain chances and the NAM keeping it\nsouth of the area and the Euro brings scattered showers north \nto the NY/PA border area Tuesday morning, but keeps it dry \nfurther north. Considering the high amount of spread and \nuncertainty in the guidance, the official forecast used the \nblended ensemble guidance, which brings a few showers southern \nand western portions of the area... and allows temperatures to \nreach the upper 50s to mid-60s for highs.\n\nFor the remainder of the week, high pressure looks like it will\nbecome established for our area. This will keep it mostly dry, \nunder northeasterly flow. Wednesday and Thursday look partly \nsunny and a bit cool with highs in the upper 50s to mid-60s. \nOvernight lows will be in the low to mid 40s each night.\n\nBy Friday, the 12Z models are keep the track of a coastal low\nfurther out into the Atlantic. As the low becomes vertically \nstacked it may stall and meander around and possibly bring\nrounds of showers into southern New England, but mostly spare\nour region.",
